I agree, and I'm (certainly by now) quite familiar with the syndrome; it
occurs in every game that extends over a significant lifespan, long
enough to establish a developed player base.  There will be a contingent
who declares the game is Perfect As Is, and that anyone else is just
going to wreck things if they go meddling.

> I think where we differ is in how its power should be reduced.  I

Or whether its power should be reduced at all.

If you reduce its power, you reduce its utility for ALL roles.  I use
Cure Sickness as an excellent example here -- for roles who have skill
in Healing spells, Cure Sickness is a better solution to "Ill" than a
unicorn horn, because it works 100% of the time on something that's
fatal if you don't fix it.  But not many roles have skill in Healing,
and Ill is a pretty critical problem.  If a unicorn horn is nerfed too
much for those roles, we are basically saying "you'll end up using the
big healing potions or dying", and that's not good; what if they don't
find the big healing potions or burn them up earlier?

I suspect that it may be more effective (and more fun for the player) to
go down the road of "improve and/or create the alternatives".  In my
work over the past couple months, I've spent a good deal of time trying
to present the player with choices; ie. "You can have this cool thing
_or_ this cool thing, but you can't have both because they take up the
amulet slot."

Consider this simple change to the unicorn horn for a moment:  What if
you could only fix bad status effects by (a)pplying it while it was wielded?

Consider also another change:  What if there were a "generalist" spell
skill that every role could get at least "basic" in... into which such
spells as Detect Foot, Cure Blindness, and Cure Sickness might fall?

Neither of these have been fully explored (in my mind, at least), but
it's paths like these that I prefer to consider, rather than directly
impairing the horn itself; I think for all its utility, the horn or
something like it is also to a degree necessary in many parts of the
game, because the game has been designed around its existence.  So,
instead, I can focus on other aspects of horn use rather than just its
effect alone.

